  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe previous solution uses primarily binary optimization with continuous polishing. To improve the bound, we need to:\n- Use continuous optimization more extensively\n- Implement simulated annealing to escape local minima\n- Search across different grid resolutions\n- Define more sophisticated neighborhood moves\n\nThis approach aims to find better minima by combining continuous optimization with global search and multiple resolutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Representation: Piecewise constant function on grid points\n- Objective: min max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over x\n- Constraints: 0\u2264h(x)\u22641, \u222bh(x)dx=1\n- Uses trust-region methods for potentially nonconvex functions\n- Evaluates correlation using FFT for efficiency\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n- Start with initial solution from provided initial_h_values\n- Use simulated annealing with grid refinement\n- Implement grid stride system for progressive optimization\n- Combine continuous and binary optimization phases\n- Carefully maintain constraint satisfaction during optimization\n- Cutoff: At 600 seconds for grid sizes >380\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n- Primary parameters: Temperature schedule, move types, neighborhood search\n- Progressive: Start with coarse grid and refine\n- Time-controlled: Give more time to finer structures\n- Employ FFT-based correlation evaluation\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n- Time estimates: 450 seconds for initial binary; 400 for continuous\n- Memory: Use arrays of acceptable growth\n- Fallback: Preserve initial solution from pre-import\n- Handling bad iterations: Restart with new random perturbations\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Ensure: All outputs are binary-valid\n- Compute: Objective with exact FFT method\n- Maintain: Time monitor with 80% margin\n- Return: Best candidate found and its score",
